 ## Invariants
 
-**Frame:** Owns the ticket's start-of-work ceremony (tracker transition + branch checkout), positioned after the tracker fetch/title resolution since the branch slug isn't known any earlier. The track (Product or Project) and its subfolder are resolved once, via `patterns/context-resolution.md`, and written to `Report.md`'s `## Context` section before requirements are drafted — no later phase re-asks or re-derives this. Requirements must include both known (from ticket) and proposed (gaps found by AI — NFRs, observability, GDPR). Proposed requirements are labelled `[proposed]` and must be explicitly accepted or rejected by the developer before the phase saves. These requirements become the evaluation criteria rows in the Requirements × Options matrix during Evaluate.
+**Context Gathering:** Read-only. Resolves the ticket number or a local `DESIGN-YYYYMMDD-XXXX` ID, fetches tracker/Confluence context, and — this stage's responsibility — identifies the track (Product or Project), its subfolder, and the Stories root that follows from it, once, via `patterns/context-resolution.md`, writing it to `Report.md`'s `## Context` section — no later phase re-asks or re-derives this. Creates the story folder (under the resolved Stories root) and `design/Report.md` (so Ceremony has something to update), then reads the relevant docs under the resolved Source docs path and records each one in `Report.md`'s `## Evidence` table — the lifecycle's one context-reading pass. Writes no framing content.
 
-**Research:** `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` returns options only — no recommendation. At least one "minimal change" option and at least one industry-standard approach must be included. No judgment is made here. Open questions from `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` are triaged interactively — one at a time — as **(V) Viability** (would disqualify an option) or **(E) Evaluation-input** (affects cost/detail only). All `(V)` OQs must be resolved before Research saves. See `patterns/design-oq-triage.md`. If Frame implied more than one independent decision, this phase loops once per decision — see `patterns/multi-decision-split.md`.
+**Ceremony:** The only stage that mutates anything external. Tracker transition (assign + move to In Progress) is an intelligent checkpoint; auto-approved when unassigned-or-self-assigned, pre-work status, and In Progress is the obvious next transition, otherwise a sign-off. Branch checkout is always a sign-off — the AI proposes the exact `git checkout -b` command but never runs it; the developer confirms once they're on it.
 
-**Evaluate:** Every requirement from `00 Frame.md` and every WAF pillar must be addressed. The recommendation must be falsifiable: it states which assumption, if wrong, would change the recommendation. Open questions are resolved with the developer before Solution begins. A closing WAF assessment runs on the recommended option only. `02 Evaluate.md` is written to disk for IDE review before the WAF assessment runs. Loops per decision under the same multi-decision policy as Research.
+**Frame:** Writes `design/00 Frame.md` using what Context Gathering already resolved — no re-fetching, no re-resolving track/subfolder. Requirements must include both known (from ticket) and proposed (gaps found by AI — NFRs, observability, GDPR). Proposed requirements are labelled `[proposed]`; these are confirmed at the phase sign-off, not asked about piecemeal. Frame also has its own dedicated step checking the Problem for bundled independent decisions (`patterns/multi-decision-split.md`, Step 2): at most one decision needs no section and no stop; two or more get a `## Decisions` section, written by `@docs-writer`, and their own sign-off right there — a material structural choice, since it decides how many times Research through Solution loop, not a content detail to fold into the main sign-off. These requirements become the evaluation criteria rows in the Requirements × Options matrix during Evaluate.
 
-**Solution:** Certainty principle applies. `Architecture.md` is the authoritative integrated end-to-end solution record: it carries every settled decision from Frame, Research, Evaluate, developer clarification, WAF and review feedback, with affected flows, consequences and verification obligations. `solution/` files contain only settled decisions and their direct consequences — no rationale, rejected alternatives or open questions. `Design.md`, `Stack.md` and `Efforts.md` provide supporting detail and must not introduce a material decision absent from or conflicting with Architecture.md. Each file is written to disk for IDE review as it is completed. After `Architecture.md` is confirmed, an optional `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` read-only review gate checks structural completeness before `Design.md` is started (see `patterns/design-architect-review.md`). A WAF review gate (`patterns/waf-review.md`, target `solution`) runs after all four files are confirmed. `Efforts.md` must include an Operational Costs section (ongoing infra consumption at expected scale). Under the multi-decision policy, `Design.md` loops per decision but `Architecture.md` stays a single integrated file — one section per decision. Publish and `{{SKILL_PREFIX}}dev` consume Architecture.md as the solution source of truth.
+**Research:** Reads context from `Report.md`'s `## Evidence` table — populated once, in Context Gathering — rather than re-reading the source docs itself. `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` returns options only — no recommendation. At least one "minimal change" option and at least one industry-standard approach must be included. No judgment is made here. Open questions from `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` are proposed a V/E classification by the agent — **(V) Viability** (would disqualify an option) or **(E) Evaluation-input** (affects cost/detail only) — confirmed or corrected at the phase sign-off, not in a separate round. See `patterns/design-oq-triage.md`. If Frame's confirmed Decisions section lists more than one decision, only the content steps loop (one `design/01 Research - {Decision}.md` per decision) — the phase still closes with exactly one Sign-off → Report → Commit, per `references/00-decision-router.md`.
 
-**Review:** The AI suggests the MR title/body but does not open it. The developer opens the MR and pastes the URL. Session ends here — this is a genuine session boundary, not just a phase transition, since it waits on an out-of-band human approval.
+**Evaluate:** Every requirement from `00 Frame.md` and every WAF pillar must be addressed. The recommendation must be falsifiable: it states which assumption, if wrong, would change the recommendation. A closing WAF assessment runs on the recommended option only, and any P1 finding is carried into the phase sign-off as a named decision rather than its own stop. Loops per decision under the same multi-decision policy as Research.
 
-**Address Feedback:** Only runs if the approver raises concerns. The AI identifies which phase/step the concern traces to and suggests a named reiterate path (e.g. "Reiterate from Evaluate, Step 4"); it does not decide the fix unilaterally. The MR must be re-approved before Publish proceeds.
+**Solution:** Certainty principle applies. `Architecture.md` is the authoritative integrated end-to-end solution record: it carries every settled decision from Frame, Research, Evaluate, developer clarification, WAF and review feedback, with affected flows, consequences and verification obligations. `solution/` files contain only settled decisions and their direct consequences — no rationale, rejected alternatives or open questions. `Design.md`, `Stack.md` and `Efforts.md` provide supporting detail and must not introduce a material decision absent from or conflicting with Architecture.md. All four are drafted back to back with no stop between them; the `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` architecture review auto-triggers (no permission ask) when a boundary, contract, infra resource, or cross-container data flow changed (see `patterns/design-architect-review.md`). The WAF review gate (`patterns/waf-review.md`, target `solution`) and the design quality gate run after drafting, with any P1 or failing check carried into the one phase sign-off — which also asks the developer to fill in `Efforts.md`'s Estimate column, since that is the one thing only they can supply. An operational-cost model is one of `interfaces/efforts.md`'s optional ways to express the design, included when it adds value, not a required section. Under the multi-decision policy, `Design.md` loops per decision but `Architecture.md` stays a single integrated file — one section per decision. Publish and `{{SKILL_PREFIX}}dev` consume Architecture.md as the solution source of truth.
 
-**Publish:** The AI does not approve its own gate — a human must approve the MR out-of-band, with no unresolved concerns, before this phase runs. `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` identifies `{{DESIGN_DOCS_ROOT}}` docs that need updating; `@docs-writer` applies the updates and `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` reviews (max 2 passes). After that review is clean, a single wrap-up stop asks one question — publish the implementation tickets from `Efforts.md` to the tracker? — then ticket publication (delegated to `@scrum-master` if yes), archive, and the design ticket's tracker sync (worklog effort derived from the design metrics + transition to Done, delegated to `@scrum-master` by state name) run straight through to a one-message close-out with the suggested commit.
+**Review:** The AI suggests the MR title/body but does not open it. The developer opens the MR and pastes the URL. Session ends here — this is a genuine session boundary, not just a phase transition, since it waits on an out-of-band human approval. Confirming that approval is Address Feedback's opening concern, not this phase's.
+
+**Address Feedback:** Always the next invocation after Review parks for approval — its own Step 1 is what confirms the outcome (**approved**, **concerns**, or **declined**), so this phase decides whether there's anything for it to do rather than Review deciding that for it. **Approved** skips this phase and continues to Publish; **declined** skips this phase and Publish, routing straight to Completion. Only on **concerns** does it actually run: the AI identifies which phase/step the concern traces to and suggests a named reiterate path (e.g. "Reiterate from Evaluate, Step 4"); it does not decide the fix unilaterally. The same three-way question is asked again after each rework round: **re-approved** (continue to Publish), **still concerns** (loop), or **declined**. A decline records the reason in `Report.md`'s `## Gate` section, skips Publish entirely (no permanent docs to update), and routes straight to Completion for an archive under `declined/` instead of `approved/`.
+
+**Publish:** The AI does not approve its own gate — a human must approve the MR out-of-band, with no unresolved concerns, before this phase runs (and it is skipped entirely for a declined design). `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` identifies `{{DESIGN_DOCS_ROOT}}` docs that need updating — auto-approved when every row is unambiguous, otherwise a sign-off; `@docs-writer` applies the updates and `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` reviews (max 2 passes) before the one phase sign-off.
+
+**Completion:** The final stage, and also directly invocable for a design whose docs landed (or that was declined) but was never archived. Branches on the gate's recorded outcome: **approved** publishes the WBS from `Efforts.md` to the tracker (auto-approved when unambiguous), archives to `{{DESIGN_STORIES_ROOT}}approved/[{TICKET}] {name}`, syncs the tracker to Done, and hands over the commit plus suggested PR title/body. **Declined** skips ticket publication, archives to `{{DESIGN_STORIES_ROOT}}declined/[{TICKET}] {name}`, syncs the tracker to its rejected/won't-do status, and ends with a neutral close — no PR, since no permanent docs were ever updated. Either way the worklog effort is derived from the design metrics, delegated to `@scrum-master` by state name, and recorded before the one phase sign-off.
 
